@page "/exsheet/formulas"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Sheet
@using ExSheet.Engine

<DocsPage Product="ExSheet" Title="Formulas"
          Lead="Formulas in Excel's syntax, computed by a declared set of functions that each give Excel's answer. Outside that set, the Sheet says it cannot rather than guess.">

    <DocsSection Title="Typing a Formula">
        <p>
            Text beginning with <code>=</code> is a Formula; anything else is a constant, read under the Sheet's
            culture — <code>1,250.50</code>, <code>4.25%</code> or <code>9/26/2026</code> under en-US. A date or a
            percentage typed as one gives a General cell that format, as in Excel. Formulas are written the same way
            in every culture: <code>,</code> between arguments, <code>.</code> as the decimal separator, English
            function names. The operators are Excel's: <code>+ - * / ^ &amp; %</code> and the comparisons
            <code>= &lt;&gt; &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Enter commits and Escape cancels. A Formula the Sheet cannot read is not committed: the edit stays open,
            and the Sheet says what is wrong. An edit opened with F2 and not typed into closes when its row is
            scrolled far out of view, as ExGrid's does, once the browser has confirmed nothing was typed into it; once
            you type, even as the Sheet scrolls, it stays open until you commit or cancel. Tokens are written in
            Excel's spelling, so <code>=sum(a1,2)</code> is
            kept as <code>=SUM(A1,2)</code>. Recalculation reaches only what a change affects, and Values are
            published only from a completed recalculation.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(Formulas)" Mud="typeof(FormulasMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/RegionalSales.cs"])">
            Select a total to read its Formula in the Formula Bar, press F2 to edit it, change a quarter's figure and watch the totals, the
            status and the lookup follow, or type another region into B7. Column E, from row 8, shows what the engine refuses.
            To insert a Reference with Point, type <code>=1+</code> in an edit and immediately press another cell.
            Its Reference stays in the Formula after the press is released; fresh typing replaces it normally.
            You can also type <code>=A1+</code> in the cell, immediately press the Formula Bar and
            continue with <code>B1</code>: both surfaces keep <code>=A1+B1</code>.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Arrays that spill">
        <p>
            A Formula whose result is several Values spills, as in Excel 365. The Formula's cell, the Anchor, shows the
            first Value, and the others fill the cells below and to the right, its Spill Range. A Reference to several
            cells spills (<code>=A2:A4</code>), and so does an operator applied to a range, element by element
            (<code>=B2:E2*1.1</code>). So do <code>FILTER</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>SORT</code>,
            <code>SORTBY</code>, <code>SEQUENCE</code> and <code>TRANSPOSE</code>, an <code>INDEX</code> with a row or
            a column of 0, and an <code>XLOOKUP</code> whose return array is several columns wide. A function that
            reduces an array takes one and returns one Value: <code>=SUMPRODUCT((A2:A4="North")*F2:F4)</code>.
            <code>INDEX</code> over several rows and columns with <code>column_num</code> left out is
            <code>#REF!</code>, as in Excel, over a Spill Range such as <code>A1#</code> too; write
            <code>=INDEX(G1:H5,2,0)</code> for the row.
        </p>
        <p>
            The cells of a Spill Range hold no Entry. When one of them holds an Entry or another Formula's spill, or
            the range would pass the Sheet's edge, nothing spills and the Anchor shows <code>#SPILL!</code>: nothing is
            overwritten, and clearing the obstacle lets the Formula spill. A Formula that reads a cell of the Spill
            Range follows it as it is blocked and freed, through a Reference that <code>INDIRECT</code> or
            <code>OFFSET</code> computes as well as a typed one. <code>A1#</code> names the Spill Range of
            the Formula in A1. Selecting a spilled cell shows its Anchor's Formula in the Formula Bar, dimmed, because
            it is edited at the Anchor. The <code>@@</code> operator is refused when a Formula is typed: no Formula
            silently takes one Value of a range.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Supported functions">
        <p>
            Each function gives Excel's result. A name outside this set is <code>#NAME?</code>, never an
            approximation of what the function might have meant. Where Excel has the function, that
            <code>#NAME?</code> is ExSheet's refusal, which no Formula catches; a name Excel does not have either is
            Excel's own <code>#NAME?</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The engine supports @DeclaredFunction.All.Count functions. Each one, with its arguments as Excel writes them
            and what it returns, is on the <a href="exsheet/functions">Functions</a> page, by category, with a filter
            and the functions that are not supported yet.
        </p>
        <p>
            Where the engine cannot give Excel's answer, it gives an Error Value instead of a different answer.
            Such a <code>#VALUE!</code> is a refusal, and nothing turns it into an answer: not <code>IFERROR</code>,
            <code>ISERROR</code>, <code>COUNT</code> or any other function around it, and not a Formula in another
            cell that reads it, directly, through a range, <code>INDIRECT</code>, a Spill Range or a Linked Table,
            which is refused in turn, as one that reads a circular reference is <code>#CIRC!</code>. Excel's own
            <code>#VALUE!</code>, such as <code>=1+"a"</code>'s, is caught as in Excel. A copy writes a refused
            cell as <code>#VALUE!</code>, which another program reads as Excel's Error Value. Pasted back into the
            Sheet it was copied from, a copy of one range brings its Formula; any other paste of a copy that holds
            one, of several ranges, with the headers, or into another Sheet on the page, is refused rather than
            pasted as a value. The Selection Summary shows no figure over a refused cell, and says why.
        </p>
        <p>The refusals, and the other Error Values the engine gives in place of an answer:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b><code>#CIRC!</code></b> is shown by every cell of a circular reference and every Formula that reads
                one, where Excel shows 0. <code>IFERROR</code> does not catch it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A function given a range where it takes one Value.</b> Excel 365 applies such a function to each
                Value and spills the results, so <code>=ROUND(B2:E2, -3)</code> gives four. ExSheet does not yet: the
                Formula is <code>#VALUE!</code>, and nothing around the call turns that into an answer, so
                <code>=IFERROR(ROUND(B2:E2, -3), 0)</code> is <code>#VALUE!</code> too.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>An array too large to hold.</b> An array of more than 2<sup>24</sup> Values, sixteen whole columns,
                is not built: whatever would build one is <code>#NUM!</code>. Excel runs out of resources before it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>XLOOKUP</code>'s and <code>XMATCH</code>'s binary search</b> (<code>search_mode</code> 2 and
                -2) answers only over a lookup array sorted as the mode says; otherwise it is <code>#VALUE!</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>An Error Value among <code>SORT</code>'s or <code>SORTBY</code>'s keys</b> sorts after numbers,
                text and booleans, and before a blank, and descending before numbers, text and booleans, as Excel
                sorts it: <code>=SORT(A1:A2)</code> over <code>#N/A</code> and 7 shows 7 first. Two different Error
                Values sort as Excel's <code>#DIV/0!</code> before <code>#N/A</code> where the order
                <code>ERROR.TYPE</code> numbers them in and the order of their texts agree; where they do not, and
                for two different ones in a descending sort, the Formula is <code>#VALUE!</code> until Excel has
                been asked. A <code>sort_index</code> that is not a whole number is truncated, as Excel's is:
                <code>=SORT(G1:H5,1.5)</code> sorts by the first column.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A blank among <code>SORT</code>'s or <code>SORTBY</code>'s keys</b> sorts after numbers, text and
                booleans, in either order, and in <code>UNIQUE</code> a blank is one of a kind only with another blank;
                it spills as 0. Excel's <code>SORT</code> was seen to sort it after numbers and text in either order
                and after a boolean ascending, its <code>SORTBY</code> after a number ascending, and its
                <code>UNIQUE</code> to count it one of a kind only with another blank, and both <code>SORT</code> and
                <code>UNIQUE</code> spilled it as 0; the rest is ExSheet's own reading until Excel has been asked.
                An Error Value key sorts before it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A blank <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>SORT</code>, <code>SORTBY</code>, <code>FILTER</code> or
                <code>TRANSPOSE</code> keeps, read inside a Formula.</b> <code>COUNTA</code> counts it and
                <code>INDEX</code> takes it out as a blank. Excel was seen to count it with <code>COUNTA</code>
                in <code>SORT</code>'s and <code>UNIQUE</code>'s array, to find it blank with <code>ISBLANK</code>
                once <code>INDEX</code> took it out of <code>SORT</code>'s, and equal to <code>""</code> once taken
                out of <code>UNIQUE</code>'s; the rest, the other three included, is ExSheet's own reading until
                Excel has been asked. The aggregates pass over it as over a blank cell, as Excel's do:
                <code>=AVERAGE(FILTER(A2:A9, B2:B9="North"))</code> over a blank averages the numbers alone, and
                <code>COUNT</code> does not count it. <code>COUNTA</code> counts it even taken out on its own,
                <code>IF</code> hands it on as it is, and <code>SORT</code> sorts it last as a blank key. A function
                whose readings of it part, such as <code>LEN</code> or <code>MATCH</code> over the array, makes the
                Formula <code>#VALUE!</code>, which <code>IFERROR</code> does not catch.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>#GETTING_DATA</code></b>, a Linked Table's data still on its way, is not an error to
                <code>IFERROR</code> and <code>ISERROR</code>: a fallback never stands in for data that has not
                arrived. See <MudLink Href="exsheet/linked-tables">Linked Tables</MudLink>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>ERROR.TYPE</code></b> numbers Error Values as Excel does: Microsoft's table, 1 to 7, and
                Excel's newer ones, such as <code>#SPILL!</code> 9 and <code>#CALC!</code> 14. It gives
                <code>#GETTING_DATA</code> and <code>#CIRC!</code> back instead of a number, so that nothing shows a
                figure while its data is on its way.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>SUBTOTAL</code> and <code>AGGREGATE</code></b> answer while a Sheet cannot hide a row, so
                <code>SUBTOTAL</code>'s 101 to 111 give what 1 to 11 give, as Excel's do when nothing is hidden. A cell
                whose Formula calls either anywhere — inside a larger expression, under <code>IF</code>, or in the
                Anchor of the Spill Range it belongs to — is nested, as Excel counts one: <code>SUBTOTAL</code> and
                <code>AGGREGATE</code>'s options 0 to 3 pass over it, and options 4 to 7 count its value. An
                <code>AGGREGATE</code> function number for a function the Sheet does not have, such as 13,
                <code>MODE.SNGL</code>, is <code>#VALUE!</code>. Given text or a boolean in place of its array,
                <code>AGGREGATE</code>'s <code>LARGE</code> and <code>SMALL</code> find no number, as Excel's do:
                <code>=AGGREGATE(14,6,"5",1)</code> is <code>#NUM!</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Arguments that take only a Reference.</b> <code>SUBTOTAL</code>'s references, <code>RANK.EQ</code>'s
                <code>ref</code> and <code>AGGREGATE</code>'s fifth and later arguments refuse, as the Formula is typed,
                what can never be a Reference — a number, a text, an operator's result such as <code>A1:A3*1</code>, a
                call such as <code>SEQUENCE(3)</code> — as Excel refuses <code>=SUBTOTAL(9,5)</code>.
                <code>INDIRECT</code>, <code>OFFSET</code>, <code>INDEX</code>, <code>CHOOSE</code>, <code>IF</code> and
                their kin are taken, since they can give one.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>LARGE</code> and <code>SMALL</code>'s k.</b> A k that is not whole is rounded up by
                <code>LARGE</code> and truncated by <code>SMALL</code>, as Excel does: <code>=LARGE(A1:B5,1.5)</code>
                is the 2nd largest, and <code>=SMALL(A1:A9,1.5)</code> the smallest. A k below 1 or above the count
                of the numbers is <code>#NUM!</code>, whole or not: <code>=SMALL(A1:A9,9.5)</code> over nine numbers
                is <code>#NUM!</code>. <b><code>MOD</code></b>'s remainder is exact, as Excel's is:
                <code>=MOD(1,0.1)</code> is 0.09999999999999995, where
                <code>number - divisor*INT(number/divisor)</code> in doubles would give 0. Where the divisor is
                added to a remainder of the other sign, the sum is held at 64 bits before it is stored, which gives
                Excel's <code>=MOD(-0.087626,6630)</code>, 6629.9123739999995, and a sum stored as the divisor is 0,
                as Excel's <code>=MOD(-1E-20,1)</code> is. The sum is held as an x87 register holds it, rounded to
                nearest, and is 0 wherever the double stored is the divisor, which gives every answer Excel was seen
                to give: <code>=MOD(-1E-17,1)</code> is 0, <code>=MOD(-0.084474,2270)</code> 2269.915526 and
                <code>=MOD(-0.024901,2350)</code> 2349.975099. It is <code>#NUM!</code> from a quotient of
                1,125,900,000,000, as Excel 365's.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>INDIRECT</code></b> reads A1-style text naming a cell or a range of the same Sheet. Like
                <code>OFFSET</code> it is recalculated after every change, and its text is only text: inserting a row
                rewrites the References beside it, never the text. It also reads what Excel was seen to read: R1C1
                text (<code>a1</code> FALSE) naming a cell or a range, <code>R3C2</code> or <code>R2C2:R5C2</code>,
                counted from the Formula's own cell by offsets in brackets, <code>R[1]C[-1]</code>, or from its own
                row or column where R or C has no number, <code>RC[-1]</code>; naming whole rows and columns,
                <code>R3</code>, <code>C[-1]</code> or <code>R2:R3</code>; or a Spill Range, <code>R1C1#</code>.
                It reads a Linked Table's column as text, <code>Positions[PV]</code>, which waits while the table
                does, and a Spill Range, <code>A1#</code>. R1C1 text that joins a cell to a whole column is the
                cell's row across the columns they span, <code>R2C2:C3</code> is B2:C2, and a whole row joined to a
                cell the rows they span down its column, <code>R3:R2C2</code> is B2:B3, as in Excel. Parts are joined
                left to right, so <code>R2:R3C2:C3</code> is B2:C3. An offset past the Sheet's edge wraps round to the
                far side, as Excel's does: from C2, <code>R[-5]C[-1]</code> is B1048573. R1C1 text that joins a cell to
                a whole row, a whole column to a cell, or a row to a column, <code>R2C2:R3</code>, <code>C3:R2C2</code>
                or <code>R3:C2</code>, names no place and is <code>#REF!</code>, as in Excel; so is a row or column
                number 0, <code>R0C2</code>, or one past the Sheet's edge, <code>R1048577C2</code>, as A1 text past
                the edge, <code>XFE1</code>, is; so is text naming another Sheet or a table that does not exist, as
                Excel's A1 text is; and so, in either style, is <code>#</code> after a range, a row or a column,
                <code>A1:A2#</code>, <code>R3#</code> or <code>R2C2:R3#</code>, or inside a join,
                <code>A1#:A2</code> or <code>R1C1#:R2C1</code>. A Linked
                Table's name on its own, <code>"Positions"</code>, is
                <code>#VALUE!</code>, though Excel reads the table's data: a Linked Table is read by its columns
                and by key, never by position, so that a Formula never reads a row by where it stands. Typed in a
                Formula, the name on its own is <code>#NAME?</code>, which no Formula catches.
                What <code>INDIRECT</code> or <code>OFFSET</code> reaches counts as a Reference typed in the Formula
                would: one that reaches its own cell or a circular reference is <code>#CIRC!</code>, and one that
                reaches data still on its way waits, even under <code>COUNT</code> or an <code>AGGREGATE</code>
                option that passes over errors. One that reaches into a Spill Range follows the spill as it is
                blocked and freed.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>LET</code></b> takes the names Excel's keyboard accepts: a letter of any script or
                <code>_</code>, then letters, digits and <code>_</code>, at most 249 characters, and at most 126
                of them. The name of a function is a name too, whether this engine has the function or not
                (<code>sum</code>, <code>irr</code>, <code>n</code>), and so are <code>R1C1</code> and <code>RC</code>;
                a name followed by <code>(</code> is still the function's call. A value may be left empty:
                <code>=LET(x,,x)</code> shows 0, as it does in Excel. A name that reads as a cell, such as
                <code>A1</code> or <code>R2</code>, and <code>TRUE</code> are refused as the Formula is typed, as a
                Formula that cannot be read is, because Excel's keyboard refuses them; so are a name bound twice and
                a missing calculation. A binding is computed only when its name is read,
                but the <code>LET</code> still depends on everything its text names, so an unused binding over
                a cycle shows <code>#CIRC!</code> and one over a Linked Table still on its way waits.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>RAND</code> and <code>RANDBETWEEN</code></b> draw from the Sheet Seed. Each draw is made from
                the seed, the Sheet's name and the cell, which call in its Formula it is and how many changes the
                Workbook has had since it was opened, so every edit, insertion and deletion on any Sheet, every Sheet
                added, deleted, renamed, moved or copied, and every undo, redo, Linked Table snapshot and Linked Table
                declaration draws again on every Sheet, as Excel's recalculation does,
                while the clock, a format or a width draws nothing. Unlike Excel, nothing draws without a change.
                An undo and a redo draw again rather than bringing back the draws they undo, as Excel's do; a
                rename draws again, as Excel's does, and so do its undo and redo. A snapshot draws again even
                when its rows are the ones the table already held, as Excel's Refresh All does with the file
                unchanged. <code>RANDBETWEEN</code>
                refuses a fractional bound, and one past 2<sup>53</sup>, with <code>#VALUE!</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>PV</code>, <code>FV</code> and <code>XNPV</code></b> give, to the last digit, every result Excel
                was seen to give for them, Error Values included. 1+rate is raised to the number of periods by
                squaring to a whole number, as 1 over the power to a negative one, by the square root at half a
                period, and otherwise as e<sup>n·ln(1+rate)</sup> with the exponential and the logarithm correctly
                rounded, which ExSheet computes the same on every platform. A negative 1+rate has an odd root, as
                <code>(-8)^(1/3)</code> does, and no other fractional power (<code>#NUM!</code>); at a rate of -1 no
                periods is <code>#NUM!</code> and a negative number of periods <code>#DIV/0!</code>, as Excel gave
                them. <code>PV</code> at a rate of -1 with periods is <code>#DIV/0!</code>, as Excel's is.
                <code>type</code> is read as a truth value: any number but 0, 0.5 and 2 as well as 1, is
                payment at the start of each period, as Excel reads it. <code>XNPV</code> counts the years as the days
                over 365 and sums from the first value, the plain sum even where its last addition nearly cancels, as
                Excel's is. A rate of 0 or below is <code>#NUM!</code>, as Excel answers. Where more than one thing
                fails — an Error Value in the rate, the values or the dates, a rate of 0 or below, counts that differ,
                a date before the first — the answer is Excel's where its observed answers settle the order it checks
                them in, as a rate that is an Error Value before anything among the values, and <code>#VALUE!</code>
                where they leave it open, as an Error Value among the dates after one among the values. A cell of the
                values or the dates that is not a number, whether text, a truth value or blank, is
                <code>#VALUE!</code> until Excel has been asked, unless a check before it answers.
                <b><code>PMT</code></b>, where it answers, gives the exact payment, rounded once, at the end of each
                period and at the start; it reads
                <code>type</code> as <code>PV</code> and <code>FV</code> do. Excel's own payment can differ from
                ExSheet's in its last digits: of the 30 payments Excel was asked, 23 differ by one to eleven units in
                the last of a double's digits, a relative difference of at most about 1.3·10<sup>-15</sup>, which at
                two of them shows in the 15th significant digit. No evaluation found gives every payment Excel gave,
                and this is a known limitation of 0.1.0. Where the evaluations Excel's payments leave give different
                kinds of answer, or answers far apart, <code>PMT</code> is <code>#VALUE!</code> until Excel has been
                asked: where a number it works with would pass a double's range or fall below its normal numbers,
                as where 1+rate raised falls below them, in <code>=PMT(-0.99,160,1000,500)</code>, and where the
                evaluations would lose more than a few of their last digits, as where the present value grown and the
                future value nearly cancel (<code>=PMT(0.1,2,1000,-1210)</code>) or a number of periods is tiny at the
                start of each period. A rate of -1 or below, and no periods at the start of each period, are
                <code>#NUM!</code>, as Excel answers, and <code>=PMT(1,1020,1000)</code> is -1000, as Excel's is,
                though a number it works with passes a double's range.
                <b><code>NPV</code></b> is <code>#NAME?</code>, which no Formula catches, as <code>IRR</code>'s is: its last digits differ from
                Excel's, and no evaluation found gives every result Excel was seen to give.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>TEXT</code></b> reads its code in the invariant spelling and shows what a cell in that format
                would show, under the Sheet's culture. <code>General</code>, and <code>@@</code> alone, fit a number to 11
                characters, as a wide column's General does, and a minus sign is not one of them:
                <code>TEXT(-1/3,"General")</code> is <code>-0.333333333</code>. A code a cell format does not have is
                <code>#VALUE!</code>,
                though Excel answers it: a fraction such as <code># ?/?</code>, or a condition such as
                <code>[&lt;=9]</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A serial below 0</b> under <code>DATEDIF</code>, <code>YEARFRAC</code>, <code>HOUR</code>,
                <code>MINUTE</code> and <code>SECOND</code> is <code>#NUM!</code>, -0.5 included, as Excel answers: it
                is not cut down to serial 0, so <code>HOUR(-0.5)</code> is not 12. <code>EDATE</code> from serial 0
                counts day 0 as <code>DATE</code> does, as Excel's does: <code>=EDATE(0,2)</code> is 60, the day that
                never existed. A boolean typed as a date of <code>EDATE</code>, <code>EOMONTH</code>,
                <code>NETWORKDAYS</code> or <code>WORKDAY</code>, or as the months, is <code>#VALUE!</code>, which
                <code>IFERROR</code> catches.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A refused Formula that would spill.</b> Where its text settles the shape of its array, as
                <code>=B1:B3*2</code> over a refused B2 does, every cell the array covers is refused with it, so a
                Formula that reads one is refused too; where something stands in the way, it is <code>#SPILL!</code>. Where the shape could depend on a Value, as with <code>FILTER</code>,
                <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>SORT</code>, <code>SEQUENCE</code>, <code>LET</code> or
                <code>=LEN(A1:A3)</code>, nothing spills, and the cells its array would cover read as blank: a known
                limitation of 0.1.0.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Title="A seeded simulation" Builtin="typeof(SeededDraws)" Mud="typeof(SeededDrawsMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/DiceRolls.cs"])">
            <code>Seed</code> fixes the Sheet Seed, so the same draws show whenever the document is opened: reload the
            page and they are back. Type into any cell and every draw changes, together with everything that reads it;
            undo, and they change again. Without <code>Seed</code>, ExSheet chooses a seed each time it opens a document;
            a prerendered page chooses none, and its drawing cells show <code>#GETTING_DATA</code> until the page is
            interactive, as <code>TODAY()</code> waits for the browser's time zone, so no draw changes with nothing done.
            Neither the seed nor a draw is saved in the Sheet Document.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Completion, hints and pointing">
        <p>While a Formula is typed, in a cell or in the Formula Bar, the Sheet helps as Excel does:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Completion.</b> Once a letter is typed, matching function names — and the names of Linked Tables —
                are listed; Tab accepts. After <code>Table[</code> the table's columns are listed. An argument that
                takes one of a fixed list, such as <code>XLOOKUP</code>'s <code>match_mode</code>, lists its values
                with Excel's texts, and Tab writes the value.
            </li>
            <li><b>The argument hint</b> shows the function's arguments, with the one the caret is in.</li>
            <li>
                <b>Pointing.</b> Wherever a Reference can go — after <code>=</code>, an operator, <code>(</code> or
                <code>,</code> — the arrow keys and the mouse point at cells and write their Reference. Shift and an
                arrow, or a drag, writes a range, and Shift+Home a range to the row's first column. Ctrl and an arrow
                points at the edge of the data, as it moves there with no edit open — <code>=</code> then Ctrl+↓ on an
                empty column writes <code>=D1048576</code> — and Ctrl+Shift and an arrow writes the range from the cell
                being edited to that edge. The Reference being written stands on a grey ground in the surface the
                edit is in — straight after <code>=</code> too, as Excel draws <code>=D11</code> — and it is a look,
                not a selection: a key typed next follows it. Typed into the
                Formula Bar, the arrow keys move the caret instead, as they do in Excel's bar, until <kbd>F2</kbd>
                points. F4 cycles the Reference at the caret through <code>$A$1</code>, <code>A$1</code>,
                <code>$A1</code> and <code>A1</code>; in a range typed out only the end at the caret cycles, and its
                column letters are written in upper case, while a range being pointed at cycles whole. Where the caret
                stands inside the text, the rewritten Reference is left selected — from its cell part, after any
                Sheet qualifier such as <code>Sheet2!</code> — so the next F4 cycles it again; where the caret stands
                at the text's end, however it got there, the caret stays after it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Reference outlines.</b> Each Reference in the Formula being edited wears a colour, and the cells it
                names are outlined on the Sheet in that colour.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Try it in the sheet above: select an empty cell, type <code>=SU</code>, press Tab, then press ↑ to point
            at a cell and Shift+↑ to make it a range.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The engine on its own">
        <p>
            <code>ExSheet.Engine</code> is the Formula engine with no UI and no dependency. Use it to compute a saved
            Sheet Document on a server, to validate one, or to build a document in code — every Example on this site
            builds its document with it. A <code>Workbook</code> holds the Sheets and everything that changes them:
            <code>new Workbook(culture)</code> starts one with a single Sheet, <code>Sheet1</code>, and
            <code>workbook.Do("Sheet1", SheetEdit.Enter(address, text))</code> reads text exactly as a user's typing is
            read. A <code>Sheet</code> is read: <code>GetDisplay</code> gives what a cell shows, formatted under the
            Sheet's culture.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(EngineOnly)" CodeOpen="true" />
        <p>
            Every operation a user can perform is a <code>SheetEdit</code> — insert or delete rows and columns, set
            a Cell Format, paste, fill — and <code>Workbook.Do(sheet, edit)</code> returns the one
            <code>WorkbookStep</code> that undoes and redoes it. A paste that writes in parts is still one edit: a copied block over several ranges
            (<code>SheetEdit.Paste(block, targets)</code>), and pasted text some of whose fields carry an exact
            number (<code>SheetEdit.PasteText(fields, entries)</code>). Each is one step, and one change, so
            <code>RAND</code> draws once for it. <code>Workbook.Check(sheet, edit)</code> says whether an edit would be refused,
            without doing it. <code>GetDisplay(address, width)</code> gives what the cell shows in a column so many characters wide:
            General rounds to fit, and any other format that does not fit is <code>####</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <code>Workbook</code> holds several Sheets, in order, under one culture. A Formula on one reads
            another's cells by naming it, <code>=Rates!A1</code> or <code>=SUM('Q1 Sales'!B2:B9)</code>, and a
            change on any Sheet recalculates what reads it on every Sheet, the new Values published together once
            the whole recalculation is done. A name no Sheet of the Workbook has is <code>#REF!</code>, as in
            Excel. The step <code>Workbook.Do(sheet, edit)</code> returns carries a <code>WorkbookChange</code>,
            which says what changed on each Sheet. Linked Tables are
            declared on the Workbook, and read from any of its Sheets. <code>ToDocument()</code> gives the
            Workbook Document, and <code>ToDocument(sheet)</code> one Sheet as a Sheet Document — every Sheet in order, the Sheet shown, the culture and the Linked Tables'
            declarations, never a Value — and <code>Workbook.Open</code> opens either again;
            <code>WorkbookDocument.FromJson</code> reads a Sheet Document too, as a Workbook of that one Sheet,
            and refuses a version it does not know or anything it does not understand. <code>SheetEdit.InsertSheet</code>,
            <code>Rename</code>, <code>MoveSheet</code>, <code>CopySheet</code> and <code>DeleteSheet</code> change
            the Workbook's Sheets, each one step that undoes it exactly: a new Sheet takes the next free
            <code>SheetN</code>, a copy takes <code>Name (2)</code>, a rename rewrites every Formula that names
            the Sheet, and a deletion turns every Reference to it into <code>#REF!</code> and gives them back
            when it is undone. A name another Sheet has, without regard to case, is refused, and so is deleting
            the last Sheet. <code>WorkbookChange.ChangesSheets</code> says that the Sheets themselves changed.
            Inserting or deleting rows or columns on one Sheet rewrites the References to it on every Sheet,
            as on its own: <code>Rates!A2</code> follows its cell down when a row is inserted above it, and
            becomes <code>Rates!#REF!</code> when its row is deleted, given back exactly by the undo. A block
            copied on one Sheet and pasted on another shifts its relative References, as Excel's copy does.
            ExSheet shows a Workbook with a tab for each Sheet: see <a href="exsheet/workbook">Workbook</a>.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(EngineWorkbook)" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

